# SYNOPSIS

```text
/proc/<pid>/status
/proc/<pid>/args
/proc/<pid>/ctl
/proc/<pid>/note
/proc/<pid>/notepg
/proc/trace/ctl
/proc/trace/kernel.trace
/proc/trace/procs
/proc/cpu
```

# DESCRIPTION

The proc service serves a directory for each process under the job its manifest grants with `jobs` (manifest(6)): the launcher's, which holds every service and everything each one starts.
A directory is named by the process's koid, its pid here, and the tree is brought up to date as it is walked or listed, so a process that has ended is gone from it.
Manifests mount it at `/proc`.

Each process's directory holds:

: `status`
  One line, as Plan 9's: the process's name, its user and its state, padded to 27, 27 and 11 columns, then nine numbers of 11: milliseconds in user mode, in the system and in real time, the same three for its children, its memory in KiB, and its base and current priority.
  The state is `New` before it starts, `Running` while it runs.
  The memory is what the process alone holds plus its share of what it shares.
: `args`
  Its name.
: `ctl`
  Takes `kill`, which ends the process.
: `note`
  A note written here is posted to the process (note(2)): a process that takes its notes gets it, and one that doesn't dies of it.
  A trailing newline is dropped, so `echo interrupt` posts `interrupt`.
: `notepg`
  A note written here is posted to the process's note group: every process in its job and in the jobs under that.

## The trace

Given the tracing resource (its manifest's `tracing`), the service also serves `trace`, after Plan 9's `/proc/trace`: the kernel's trace (trace(6)) with its samples of what each CPU runs, and what a reader needs to name the samples' addresses.
trace(8) records through it, and vx-trace(1)'s `profile` reads what it copies.

: `trace/ctl`
  Takes `start [hz <n>] [pages <n>] [<category> ...]` and `stop`.
  `start` records the kernel's samples, <n> a second (1000 by default, at most 100000), each a thread's PC and up to 16 return addresses, and the records of each <category> named: `sched`, `irq`, `vm`, `ipc`, `futex`, `syscall`.
  Each CPU keeps <pages> × 128 records (512 by default, at most 4096); once its ring is full it keeps none, and counts those it drops.
  A sample takes a record, and one more for each two return addresses.
  A `start` while it records ends that recording first, and empties the files below; `stop` without one is refused.
  Reading it says `idle`, `recording hz <n>` and the categories, or `stopped hz <n>`.
: `trace/kernel.trace`
  Once stopped, the recording: every CPU's ring, in trace(6)'s format.
  Empty before.
: `trace/procs`
  Once stopped, every process seen from the start to the stop, those ended meanwhile too, as text: `sampling <hz>`, then for each process `process <pid> <task> <name>` and, for each of its mappings that may run, `map <pid> <base> <size> <offset> <name>`, the numbers after the pid in hexadecimal.
  <task> is what a sample's thread id says of its process (its high 20 bits; sys-process(2)'s `Thread.traceID`), <offset> where the mapping starts in its memory, and <name> that memory's: `bootfs`, a file of bootfs(4), or `push:<name>` (launcher(8)).

## The CPUs

Given the system resource's INFO range (its manifest's `info`), the service also serves `cpu`: `busy <n>`, the percentage of the online CPUs' time that wasn't idle over the last second.
Each channel opened at it reads the line at once; after that, a read waits until the percentage has moved 5 points from what that channel read last, or reached 0 or 100, so `cat /proc/cpu` prints a line at each change.
The service samples the CPUs once a second while a channel is open at the file, and not at all while none is: deskbar(4)'s gauge holds it open only while someone is at the desktop.

It also serves `sysstat`, each CPU's counts since boot read as it is opened, a line a CPU: `cpu <n> online|offline wakeups <n> interrupts <n> timer <n> resched <n> generic <n> switches <n> idle <ms>`, the device interrupts, timer interrupts, rescheduling and other IPIs (which add up to its wakeups), its context switches, and its milliseconds idle.
On an idle machine every interrupt a CPU takes wakes it, so two reads some seconds apart count an idle desktop's wakeups, by CPU and by kind.

# EXAMPLES

Interrupt process 5636:

```rc
echo interrupt >/proc/5636/note
```

Profile the node for a second, at 10 kHz:

```rc
echo start hz 10000 >/proc/trace/ctl; sleep 1; echo stop >/proc/trace/ctl
cp /proc/trace/kernel.trace /proc/trace/procs /mnt/term/root
```

# FILES

: `/proc/<pid>/status`
  The process's status line.
: `/proc/<pid>/args`
  Its name.
: `/proc/<pid>/ctl`
  Commands: `kill`.
: `/proc/<pid>/note`
  Notes for the process.
: `/proc/<pid>/notepg`
  Notes for its note group.
: `/proc/trace/ctl`
  The kernel's trace: `start`, `stop`.
: `/proc/trace/kernel.trace`, `/proc/trace/procs`
  The last recording, and the processes it saw.
: `/proc/cpu`
  `busy <n>`, read when it changes.

Without the tracing resource there is no `trace`, and without the INFO range no `cpu`.

