# DIAGNOSTICS

A write to `note` fails when five notes already wait, or the process has ended; a write to `ctl` fails for any command but `kill`.
A write to `trace/ctl` fails for anything else than its two commands, a number out of range or an unknown category, and for `stop` with nothing recording; a `start` the kernel can't make rings for fails with an I/O error, and leaves it idle.

# BUGS

Plan 9's `ns`, `fd`, `wait`, `mem` and `regs` aren't served, and `ctl` takes neither `stop` nor `start`: a process's namespace and descriptors live in the process, and the kernel keeps neither its children nor its start time, so real time reads 0, as do system time and the priorities.
`args` is the name alone, for the same reason.

`trace/procs` knows the processes there at the start and at the stop: one that started and ended between them is missing, and its samples are named by their task alone.
A channel just opened at `cpu` reads the last percentage shown, which may be from before it was last closed, until the next sample a second later.

A sample is taken where the CPU next takes interrupts, so code that runs with them masked shows at its end; a stack is followed by its frame pointers, at most 16 frames.
There is one recording at a time, for whoever writes `ctl`.
